\label{thm:quadratic program} 
	Given $A\in{\mathbb R}^{m\times n}$ and $\mathbf{b}\in{\mathbb R}^m$ where all the row of $A$ has unit norm. Let $\gamma(A)\geq0$ be the niceness parameter of $A$ defined later in Definition~\ref{def:nice}. Suppose $\gamma(A)>0$. There exists a polynomial $\alpha(\cdot)$ such that for any $t\geq0$, let $\mathbf{x}(t)$ be the firing rate of a simple continuous SNN with $C=A^\top A$, $\mathbf{I}=A^\top\mathbf{b}$, $\eta=1$, and $0<\alpha\leq \alpha(\frac{\gamma(A)}{n\cdot\lambda_{\max}})$. For any $\epsilon>0$, when $t\geq\frac{\sqrt{\lambda_{\max}\cdot n}}{\epsilon\cdot\lambda_{\min}\cdot\|\mathbf{b}\|_2}$, then $\mathbf{x}(t)$ is an $\epsilon$-approximation solution to the non-negative least squares problem.

	Here, we say $\mathbf{x}$ is an $\epsilon$-approximation\footnote{The reason why we define in this way is to handle the case where the program has many solutions. In such case the only \textit{unique} thing is that $\|A\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}\|_2$ are all the same among these optimal solutions.} solution if for any optimal solution $\mathbf{x}^*$ of the above program $\|A\mathbf{x}-A\mathbf{x}^*\|_2\leq\epsilon\cdot\|\mathbf{b}\|_2$.

Given $A\in{\mathbb R}^{m\times n}$ and $\mathbf{b}\in{\mathbb R}^m$, we first define the \textit{conic projection} of $\mathbf{b}$ on the cone spanned by the column of $A$ as follows.
\begin{equation*}
\mathbf{x}^+ = arg\,min_{\mathbf{x}\in{\mathbb R}^n_{\geq0}} \|A\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}\|_2^2.
\end{equation*}
Here, $\mathbf{x}^+$ is the optimal solution of~\eqref{op:quadratic program proof} and we let $\mathbf{b}^+=A\mathbf{x}^+$ which is the conic projection of $\mathbf{b}$ on the cone spanned by the column of $A$. Note that $\mathbf{x}^+$ is also the optimal solution of the following optimization program with minimum value to be $0$.
\begin{equation}\label{op:quadratic shift}
\min_{\mathbf{x}}\|A\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{b}^+\|_2^2.
\end{equation}
Given a simple SNN with $C=A^\top A$ and $\mathbf{I}=A^\top\mathbf{b}$, the dual SNN as defined in Section~\ref{sec:dual SNN} would be
\begin{equation*}
